csv:     155mm-m107-perforation-1-8in.csv
source:  ../source.pdf p.93 = report page 79 (RIGHT table of the pair)
anchor:  TABLE 57
anchor:  PERFORATION OF
anchor:  INITIAL FRAGMENT VELOCITY 3,500 F/S
anchor:  # 155-MM N.E. SHELL, M107

# VERIFIED AGAINST THE PAGE IMAGE, 2026-08-02. All 60 cells were read off a
# 200-dpi render of source.pdf p.93 and match this CSV exactly; the caption
# "TABLE 57 / PERFORATION OF 1/8 IN. MILD STEEL" is printed above this table in
# the right half of the page. This is the largest of the six tables and the one
# whose text layer is most damaged (the OCR renders the caption "PERFORATION OF
# Vg IN. ~ILD STEEL"), which is precisely why the page image, not the text
# layer, is the authority.
#
# Criterion: PERFORATION OF 1/8-IN. MILD STEEL. No energy closure exists --
# 1/2 m v^2 runs 248-1146 ft-lb across these rows. Identity was by elimination
# against 155mm-m107-casualties.csv, and is now also direct from the page
# geometry; see 75mm-m48-perforation-1-8in.invariant for the full admissibility
# note and why monotonicity is NOT a column-identity test.
#
# CROSS-SHELL CLOSURE: (m, v) here is identical to 105mm-m1-perforation-1-8in
# on all 11 shared ranges -- both shells fire at 3,500 f/s and (m, v) is a
# single-fragment ballistics result independent of shell size. This pair closes
# exactly, unlike the casualties pair (see 155mm-m107-casualties.invariant).
# Checked by checks/ordnance-1944-page-geometry.py.
#
# This column carries one more range step (400 ft) than its casualties partner
# stops at (600 ft on a coarser grid) -- the pair goes ragged at the tail, so
# row i of one file is NOT row i of the other beyond r=200 ft.
monotonic:  r_ft increasing
monotonic:  N non-increasing
monotonic:  B non-increasing
monotonic:  m_oz non-decreasing
monotonic:  v_fps non-increasing
